  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(t) & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{e}^{t}\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8{e}^{t}\\,,\\,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $\u27e80\\,,\\,1\u27e9$ and ${r}^{\u2032}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $\u27e80\\,,\\,1\u27e9\\cdot {r}^{\u2032}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u27e80\\,,\\,1\u27e9\\cdot {r}^{\u2032}(t) & =0 \\\\ \u27e80\\,,\\,1\u27e9\\cdot \u27e8{e}^{t}\\,,\\,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}\u27e9 & =0 \\\\ 0\\cdot {e}^{t}+1\\cdot \\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ t & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=0.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\u27e8{e}^{0}\\,,\\,\\sqrt{1+{0}^{2}}\u27e9=\u27e81\\,,\\,1\u27e9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\u27e80\\,,\\,1\u27e9$ and ${r}^{\u2032}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(1,1).$",
